detail.abstract:
This working paper assembles a public-data foundation for studying airport disruption readiness. It integrates roughly 1.1 GB across twenty data batches-US BTS flight-level records, OpenSky ADS-B movements, EUROCONTROL punctuality and ATFM delay files, NOAA and NASA weather products, FAA and NTSB safety records, OpenFlights and OurAirports topology, World Bank and Eurostat panels, GDELT media data, and a Brazilian ANAC replication file-together with a separately downloaded full-year 2024 US panel of 7.08 million departures used for out-of-sample testing. Thirty-three analytical sections are reported in full, each with its methods and limitations stated.&nbsp; 
<div>
 The central finding is that physical infrastructure does not explain airport recovery. Across 53 large US airports, recovery time after a delay shock rises with airport scale rather than falling: runway count correlates positively with recovery duration (r = 0.43, 95% CI 0.18 to 0.63). The gradient replicates in full-year 2024 and holds when shock thresholds are set relative to each airport's own distribution rather than a fixed cutoff. A Cox proportionalhazards model fitted to both periods finds one stable trait-connectivity slows recovery (HR 0.81 in 2015, 0.84 in 2024)-and one stable null: shock severity does not predict recovery duration in either year. Two 2015 coefficients did not survive replication and are reported as period effects rather than dropped: the winter penalty reverses, and the low-cost-carrier association vanishes.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Airport-level proxies for operating model, carrier concentration and schedule peaking, carry no signal. The larger operating differences are between carriers, where delay performance varies roughly fourfold within the same national system. Also reported: a recovery half-life metric, delay-propagation and weather dose-response results, passenger-delay concentration, European ATFM degradation by regulation reason, MENA-specific exposure, and a composite readiness index that fails out of sample.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 No causal identification is claimed and no validated readiness index is offered. The contribution is a transparent, replicable empirical base for theory-building, policy analysis, and causal work on airport disruption using data anyone can obtain.
</div>

Purpose: Although research analyzing the consequences of salesperson social media use in driving sales behaviors and performance outcomes has proliferated in the recent past, there are significant research gaps in the domain. Grounded in task-technology fit theory, we propose a conceptual framework that integrates between previously disjointed areas of research and analyzes the relationships between salesperson social media use, brand awareness, creativity, manager empowerment, and company performance.&nbsp;
<div>
 Design/Methodology: Survey responses were collected from a multi-industry sample of 158 B2B salespeople. Structural relationships were tested using PLS-SEM. Findings: The analysis shows that salesperson social media use positively affects brand awareness. The relationship between social media and brand awareness is magnified with the increase in salesperson creativity. Findings also show that manager empowerment increases salesperson creativity. Lastly, brand awareness positively affects company performance.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Practical Implications: Sales organizations should focus on developing digital strategies, especially focusing on salesperson social media use to enhance company's brand awareness, which in turn increases company performance. Moreover, sales managers should also follow empowering leader behaviors to enhance creativity.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Originality/Value: We amalgamate salesperson social media use literature and branding literature by proposing salesperson social media use's positive effects on brand awareness. Our study also expands the knowledge by exploring the moderating effect of individual-level variables such as salesperson creativity on driving the effects of salesperson social media use.
</div>
